<HTML> 
<HEAD> 
<TITLE>Cars &amp; Car Conversions Article about a Race going FIAT Tipo 16V</TITLE> 
</HEAD> 
<BODY>

<!-- Set black background and white text.>

<BODY TEXT="#FFFFFF" BGCOLOR="#000000" LINK="#90FFFF" VLINK="#CCCCCC">
<BR><BR>

<CENTER><IMG SRC="spirito.jpg" WIDTH=700 HEIGHT=72
ALT="Spirito di Tipo">
</CENTER>

<!-- Use Arial font for whole document, if this fails use helvetica >

<FONT FACE="Arial,Helvetica">
<BR><BR>
<FONT SIZE=+2>
The 16v Tipo impressed us when it first arrived over here. Up 
against some tough opposition in the ‘ot’ atch market, the 16v 
had style and top driver appeal. But what about this racing 
version? Dr Sauer pulled on his Armani race suit and Gucci 
booties to find out.
</FONT>
<BR><BR>
<CENTER><IMG SRC="sideview.jpg" WIDTH=700 HEIGHT=283
ALT="Selenia Racing Tipo">
</CENTER>

<BR><BR>
<B><FONT SIZE=+8 COLOR="#FFFF00">W</FONT></B>
ith the current range of Fiats bringing excitement back into the 
brand, it could be argued that the Tipo 16V was the forerunner 
of this trend. Sure, there were plenty of Strada performance 
derivatives that were fast too, but hey, let’s face it. They all 
looked like designer rubbish bins on wheels. The build quality of 
some of them was not much better either (actually, that’s 
probably maligning the rubbish bins!).
<BR><BR>
Does this Fiat do any better? I think so, but read on and decide 
for yourself. Me? I’ve made up my mind, no mistake! It looks 
good, it goes well and it’s reliable. Yeah, and pigs will fly I 
suppose. There is no substitute for trying, and as the saying 
goes, we’ll try anything once.
<BR><BR>
<BR CLEAR="ALL">
<B><FONT SIZE=+2 COLOR="#FFFF00">
FIAT TIPO
</FONT></B><BR>
The sports version of the Tipo was first introduced during the 
1989 Turin Motorshow as a 1756cc 138bhp model, and became 
immediately popular with the buying public and the press alike, 
even though the car was fairly heavy and not necessarily the 
class leader on spec and performance. What it did offer was a 
lot of interior space for the money and very acceptable handling 
and good overall speed. Grip was of a high order and the 
combination of the revised body trim and the lowered ride height 
made the car look very good. The performance of the 1800cc 
engine was strong but the overall weight of the car at just under 
1200kg did tell when you were trying to go quickly.
<BR><BR>
This was put right in a thorough manner when during the 1991 
Geneva Show, Fiat announced a 2.0-litre 16v version that had 
not only more power but also delivered a lot more torque, low 
down. The new (for the Tipo) engine was able to produce almost 
80% of its maximum torque at 2500rpm. At 75bhp-per-litre the 
power was not the best in class, but as before, it is the overall 
effect of the power and torque combined that made the car fast 
in top speed and very quick in acceleration. Many of the journals 
at the time were able to squeeze 0-60mph in around 7.5sec 
from the press test cars.
<BR><BR>
Though very accomplished and well-built, the cars did not sell in 
the numbers in the UK that Fiat had hoped for, but in fairness, 
the cars were introduced during a time when the UK was going 
through the worst recession that it had experienced for a long 
time, and hot hatches were about as welcome as a plate of 
dogsick during a gourmet meal. It did acquire a good reputation 
for itself and being one of the last kids on the block Fiat had 
done its best to make the Tipo 16V a class leader, a mission in 
which it almost succeeded.
<BR><BR>
For example, one advanced feature of the engine was the 
adoption of two balance shafts fitted to the sides of the cylinder 
block to smooth out vibrations. ABS was standard, and the 
interior equipment and looks were up with the best in class, 
while for space it could beat the best.
<BR><BR>
With the introduction of the Barchetta and the Coupe, the 
Bravo/Brava, the excellent Punto GT and last, but not least, the 
fun to look at and to drive Cinquecento Sport, Fiat is clearly back 
in town with an exciting range of vehicles. Now it is up to the 
trade to tune a few to add that extra factor that can sometimes 
make for a memorable combination. Think back to the early 
days of hot hatches and how we all clamoured to drive one of 
the Brian Ricketts-developed MK1 1800 Golf GTis. That is what 
we are looking for. Fiat has put the zip back into its cars: now 
let’s find a few tuned ones to report on.
<BR><BR>
<BR CLEAR="ALL">
<B><FONT SIZE=+2 COLOR="#FFFF00">
OUR TIPO
</FONT></B><BR>
Our Tipo ended up in the Clinic because I received a cry for help 
from my friend Guy Croft, who had been asked to rebuild the 
engine and in true tradition was aware that horsepower alone is 
not the answer to a quick lap time. Indeed in many cases the 
opposite is true: more power fed into an inadequate chassis can 
mean slower lap times.
<BR><BR>
<IMG SRC="patients.jpg" ALIGN=RIGHT WIDTH=250 HEIGHT=165
ALT="Simon, Mike &amp; Guy">
This model is driven by Andy Giles who is in the computer 
business, and it belongs to <A HREF="http://www.selenia.net" target="_blank">Selenia Oils</A> and Magneti Marelli UK 
Ltd. The car was originally built from a road car by Belle Vue 
Motors, the Southend Fiat specialists, for production saloon car 
racing. More recently it has been raced in the
<A HREF="http://www.selenia.net" target="_blank">Selenia Oils</A> Fiat 
championship, and the car was in a fairly rundown state when 
Andy approached Guy with a view to rebuilding the engine and 
getting some more power from it in the process. This being one 
of Guy’s specialities, he set about doing so with his usual 
thoroughness. The chassis side of things also needed some 
attention, but not knowing where to start, Guy called me for help. 
And what better way to do this then to bring the car to the CCC 
Clinic.
<BR><BR>
As befits a Clinic session it was our objective to find out what 
sort of a base line we had, and then to make suitable 
modifications and alterations where necessary. We began by 
looking at the car’s present set-up and tried to work out the 
things that had been done already, whether they were legal, and 
whether they worked well or not. As is so often the case you end 
up doing everything again, but this time from the perspective of 
acquired knowledge of the components and the car, rather than 
guesswork. When considering the set-up and any changes 
planned, it is always useful to try and work out what sort of 
competition the car is entered into. As we have said, the Tipo is 
used in the BRSCC Selenia Motor Oil Fiat Racing Challenge 
series.
<BR><BR>
<IMG SRC="engine.jpg" ALIGN=LEFT WIDTH=250 HEIGHT=168
ALT="Engine Bay">
The Fiat’s engine has a very strong torque curve from low down, 
which is extremely useful as it races on circuits like Lydden Hill 
and Brands Hatch where the slow-corner exit, and the traction 
away from those corners, is very important for a good lap time. 
Obviously the driver must not try and overdrive into and out of 
such corners, as the attitude of the car is critical to the tyres 
being able to lay down the power from the strong and willing 
engine. Slow in and fast out is the way. Sounds obvious doesn’t 
it? But it is not so easily done when someone is breathing down 
your neck.
<BR CLEAR="ALL">
<BR><BR>
<B><FONT SIZE=+2 COLOR="#FF6600">
INTERIOR
</FONT></B><BR>
<IMG SRC="interior.jpg" ALIGN=RIGHT WIDTH=175 HEIGHT=249
ALT="Interior">
Our car is still prepared to Production Saloon spec, in that on 
the interior most of the trim must stay except the floor mats and 
the roof lining. The rest of the interior is as you would expect a 
club saloon racer to be. The racing seat and the four-point 
harness are of unspecified make but look in good condition and 
work well. The pedals are standard as are the main part of the 
gauges, except for oil pressure and temperature, and coolant 
temperature gauges, and a very large low pressure oil warning 
light stuck in the middle of the centre dash column so you 
cannot ignore it.
<BR><BR>
There is a full Safety Devices roll cage which is bolted in, but the 
shell has not been seam welded (not legal in prodsaloons). 
There is ample evidence that the cage has been moving and 
needs further anchoring to provide stiffness and protection, 
while a few larger load-spreading washers would not go amiss 
here too.
<BR><BR>
<BR CLEAR="ALL">
<B><FONT SIZE=+2 COLOR="#FF0000">
BRAKES
</FONT></B><BR>
The brakes looked and behaved as standard and at the time of 
writing there was no information available on this score, but it 
did look as though the pad material bad been up rated by the 
colour and the feedback from their material
<BR CLEAR="ALL">
<BR><BR>
<B><FONT SIZE=+2 COLOR="#3366FF">
SUSPENSION
</FONT></B><BR>
Here a lot more was known, mainly because Guy had been busy 
measuring and testing things for me so that we could make 
some intelligent decisions about, for example, shock absorber 
rates and the spring rates and wheel rates. Guy and his team of 
Simon and Mike had removed all the individual components 
from the suspension to be weighed and measured so that we 
could calculate the roll centre and the unsprung weight of the 
components for the purpose of working out the shock rates.
<BR><BR>
<IMG SRC="suspension.jpg" ALIGN=LEFT WIDTH=150 HEIGHT=201
ALT="Close up of front suspension">
At the front there are the ubiquitous MacPherson struts and 
lower triangular wishbones, a 16mm anti-roll bar, and rack-and-
pinion steering of relatively low gearing, but providing good feel. 
The car has 190 lb/in front springs of 3.73in diameter with about 
eight coils; these lower the car the maximum allowed and have 
been proven to be the best rates for front end grip.
<BR><BR>
At the rear the suspension consists of a trailing arm 
arrangement held together by a crosstube and complemented 
by an anti-roll bar some 18mm thick. The rear suspension is 
hung in a subframe-type
assembly, heavily anchored in rubber to insulate the body from 
suspension and road noise, This works well to isolate those very 
noises, but it is deadly for control and accuracy, and in this case 
it really screws up the frequency control of the rear. The 
diagonal movement across the car is also poor, and this alone 
makes for a very choppy character during cornering. This is one 
of the most lap time-destroying properties you can find in any 
car. This car had it by the bag full: so much so, that when you 
really tried to push the car into a turn you would be faced with 
severe porpoising; the whole car would have its wheels off the 
ground, like a demented horse trying to throw its rider.
<BR CLEAR="ALL">
<BR><BR>
<B><FONT SIZE=+2 FONT COLOR="#66FF99">
DRIVETRAIN
</FONT></B><BR>
<IMG SRC="bhp.jpg" ALIGN=RIGHT WIDTH=325 HEIGHT=215
ALT="Power Plot">
Here is the heart of the machine. The 4-valve engine, all 2 litres 
of it, is a true Fiat jewel and tireless worker at all revs, with 
unburstable mechanicals, especially so when Guy Croft Tuning 
have done their magic on the assembly, as had been the case 
here The engine had been stripped and rebuilt just before our 
Clinic session, and the ensuing rolling road run at Southern 
Carburettors was eagerly awaited by both ourselves and the 
GCT guys. As you can see, and bear in mind that this is a 
virtually standard unit still wearing all the inlet tract noise 
reduction equipment and trunking, the engine peaked at 
6603rpm with 179bhp at the flywheel. This is as near as damn a 
90bhp/litre engine on a single - admittedly large - throttle body, 
and the standard exhaust manifold and Marelli ignition/injection 
equipment. That is very impressive by any means of 
comparison.
<BR><BR>
The compression ratio is set to 10.5:1 and the cylinder head has 
been treated to recut and blueprinted valve seats and mild 
parting and matching of the portfaces, but still retains standard 
valve sizes. The whole engine assembly has been balanced and 
honed and fitted with a slightly lightened flywheel.
<BR><BR>
The transaxle is still as standard, although on the 16V the axle 
ratio is 3.563:1 which for the purposes of racing would really 
best be changed over for the readily-available 1.8-litre ratio of 
3.84:1. This would be more appropriate for racing. This is very 
simple and cheap to do, and would aid lap times substantially. 
The ratios in the box are reasonably spaced, with 5th gear being 
slightly overdriven at 0.942:1, but 4th not quite 1.0:1. However 
the gear ratios are quite widely spaced, meaning the engine is 
not always in the ideal rpm band; it’s just lucky that it has very 
strong torque spread
<BR><BR>
<BR CLEAR="ALL">
<B><FONT SIZE=+2 COLOR="#FFFF00">
DRIVING
</FONT></B><BR>
The car was actually disappointment to drive, from the 
perspective of the chassis preparation and condition. The 
engine screwed the hell out of the suspension and chassis 
components. When cornering hard you could hear the groaning 
noises from the roll cage and the shell. The car started to 
oscillate and this motion would culminate in a series of speed-
sapping lumps that would destroy any semblance of controlled 
progress.
<BR><BR>
It is here that the most money and attention needs to be spent 
The steering and brakes were generally good and suitable to 
race with, and the feedback from the controls, such as pedals 
and gearstick, was good too but the gear lever did suffer from an 
uncertain and offputting period between 2nd and 3rd.
<BR><BR>
<IMG SRC="weights.jpg" ALIGN=LEFT WIDTH=200 HEIGHT=251
ALT="Weight Distribution">
From the overall weight figure you can see that the car weighs 
nearly 1120kg, some 60-65kg lighter than standard and in full 
trim. However, during all our tests the car had a nearly full tank 
of fuel and that would have added some 25-30kg to the kerb 
weight It is important to remember that this obviously influences 
the acceleration runs as well. Nevertheless with the right revs at 
4500rpm from standstill and not quite dumping the clutch, the 
car did run regular low 7sec 0-60mph runs, with a best of 
7.l8sec and a tremendous 1/4 mile time of 15.79sec. The 
terminal velocity at that point was just 81.2mph. These figures 
show the strong and consistent pulling power through the range 
and must be a guide to what can really be achieved with just 
one person in the car (all our runs were with two up and 3/4 
tank). Just to put all that in perspective, the car has 6.25Kg/hp, 
which is not all that great but it is the sheer pulling grunt from the 
tremendous torque that saves it, and indeed makes it genuinely 
quick.
<BR><BR>
My feeling is that underneath this, at the present moment, 
terrible skin there is a very decent race car lurking that could in 
time be very quick, providing that it can have a weight reduction 
programme that is legal. It also needs to have its chassis and 
subframe stiffened and the roll cage more integrated into the 
structure.
<BR CLEAR="ALL">
<BR><BR>
<B><FONT SIZE=+2 COLOR="#FFFF00">
CONCLUSIONS
</FONT></B><BR>
<IMG SRC="conclusion.jpg" ALIGN=RIGHT WIDTH=225 HEIGHT=599
ALT="Engineers Report">
There is a lot of lap time in this car once the major gremlin - the 
dreaded hop - has been cured. This, with several detailed 
preparation exercises, and a bout of consistent testing will see 
the car very competitive indeed. This will cost time and money 
and the question is: is it in the best interests of
<A HREF="http://www.selenia.net" target="_blank">Selenia Oils</A> to 
support a vehicle that is no longer sold? If you are going to put 
some proper money into it you might as well spend it on a new 
car with the same, or similar, engine; but more current and 
representative of the current Fiat range. There again, that would 
be a little beyond the idea of the Fiat series itself, which is really 
for people to race their cars in a cost-controlled formula, but 
somebody has got to start using the new models sooner or later 
and it might as well be the commercial organisations doing it.
<BR><BR>
This is an example of a clubmans car that has not been loved 
and cared-for for a while, but is now in need of a major overhaul 
to attain the sort of performance that you could expect from it. 
As it is it won’t do it, but with the recommendations behind it and 
the team work to complement it the picture will change very 
quickly. Let’s hope that it is given that chance.
<BR><BR>
Thanks to <A HREF="http://www.selenia.net" target="_blank">Selenia Oils</A> for making the car available for test at 
such short notice, and also Gravesend Transmissions who 
stepped in when the gearbox needed first aid. They repaired the 
broken bits and made it possible for this Clinic to be conducted. 
Well done!!
<BR CLEAR="ALL">
<BR><BR>

